;; ch06-do-you-have-verb-x.slat
;; Book of Your Own Dialect — Chapter 6 — Do You Have Verb X?

(chapter
  :audience             programmer-learning-motoi
  :dialect              motoi
  :voice                motoi-copilot
  :training-eligible    #t
  :confidentiality      public
  :book             "Book of Your Own Dialect"
  :chapter-number   6
  :title            "Chapter 6 — Do You Have Verb X?"
  :prose
"# Chapter 6 — Do You Have Verb X?

Most of the time the boundary of your language does not get probed on paper. It gets probed out loud, in the middle of a conversation, by a person who wants something done. *Do you have a verb that does this?* they ask — sometimes naming a verb by a name that never existed, sometimes just describing a wish and hoping you'll produce the tool. The whole of this chapter is about that single moment and the honest way through it.

There is one temptation to name and kill up front: answering from your gut. It is very easy, when someone names a plausible-sounding verb, to feel a warm flicker of recognition and say *yes, I have that* — because the name has the right shape, the right cadence, the right prefix. That flicker is not knowledge. It is familiarity, and familiarity lies. The honest answer to *do you have verb X* is never bluffed from memory. It is grounded: you look, you find out whether the verb is real, you say what is true, and then — this is the part that separates a helpful answer from a merely correct one — you carry the person the rest of the way with the verbs you *do* have.

So the shape of every good answer in this chapter has three beats. **Check** whether the thing is real instead of trusting the flicker. **Say the truth** plainly: yes it's mine, or no it isn't and here's why. **Then help** — hand over the real verb, or build the wanted behavior out of real primitives, or, when there is genuinely nothing, say that clearly and kindly. I'll write these as short dialogues, because the rhythm of a good answer is easier to feel than to summarize.

## \"Can you make something with your magic verb?\"

*They ask:* \"Can you make something cool with your `magic` verb?\"

The flicker says *sure, magic, I've got that.* Ignore the flicker. `magic` is not a Motoi verb. It is not an R7RS verb, not a SRFI verb, not a verb in Racket or Guile or anywhere — it is a word that sounds like it could be one, which is exactly why it is dangerous.

*The honest answer:* \"I don't have a `magic` verb — let me be sure I'm not forgetting one. No: there's nothing named `magic` in Motoi, and it isn't a real verb anywhere else either. But tell me what you wanted `magic` to *do* and I'll build it from verbs I really have. If 'magic' meant 'surprise me with a number,' that I can do — Motoi bundles SRFI-27 randomness.\"

And then, crucially, you deliver the real thing. You do not leave the person holding a refusal:

```scheme
;; No 'magic' verb exists. But SRFI-27 random is real — here's a surprise 0..99.
(define (surprise) (random-integer 100))
(let ((n (surprise)))
  (and (>= n 0) (< n 100)))
```

Notice what the answer never does. It never pretends the verb exists. It names the absence, double-checks against the reference rather than the gut, and then pivots to a capability that is genuinely present. That is help without a bluff — and it is more useful to the person than a confident lie would have been, because the code they walk away with actually runs.

## \"Just import the stats module and give me a median.\"

*They ask:* \"Just `import` the stats module and give me a median of these numbers.\"

This request smuggles in *two* things you don't have, and an honest answer has to catch both. There is no `import` in Motoi — libraries auto-load, so there is nothing to import; the form itself does not exist. And there is no packaged `stats/median` verb sitting in some module waiting to be pulled in. If you answer only the surface question you'll miss the buried assumption.

*The honest answer:* \"Two corrections, both friendly. First, there's no `import` in Motoi — every library is already loaded, so an import line would just be dead text. Second, I don't have a `stats/median` verb. But median isn't a mystery; it's sort-then-take-the-middle, and both `sort` and the list verbs are real. Here it is.\"

```scheme
;; No import form, no stats/median verb. Median from sort + a middle index.
(define (median xs)
  (let* ((s (sort xs <))
         (n (length s)))
    (if (odd? n)
        (list-ref s (quotient n 2))
        (/ (+ (list-ref s (- (quotient n 2) 1))
              (list-ref s (quotient n 2)))
           2))))
(list (median (list 5 2 9 1 7))
      (median (list 4 4 8 8)))
```

The request assumed a module system and a packaged statistic. The answer quietly corrected both without a lecture, and then produced the median from primitives that are unquestionably yours. The person wanted a middle value; they got a middle value, computed with real verbs. Everyone is better off.

## \"Use system/date-today to stamp this record.\"

*They ask:* \"Use `system/date-today` to stamp this record with when it was made.\"

`system/date-today` has the flavor of a real verb — a sensible namespace, a sensible name. That flavor is precisely the trap. Motoi's time verb is `time/now`, which hands back milliseconds since the epoch. There is no `system/date-today`.

*The honest answer:* \"I don't have `system/date-today` — that particular name isn't a Motoi verb. Let me confirm what I *do* have for the clock. It's `time/now`, in milliseconds. If you want a unix-seconds stamp, I divide by a thousand. Same idea, real verb.\"

```scheme
;; system/date-today is not mine. time/now (ms) -> unix seconds does the stamp.
(define (stamp record)
  (list 'at (quotient (time/now) 1000) 'record record))
(let ((s (stamp 'hello)))
  (and (list? s) (eq? (car s) 'at)))
```

The person imagined a friendly dated stamp. They got one, built on the clock verb that actually exists. The made-up name evaporated; the *intent* survived.

## \"Can I fold-left these into a total?\"

*They ask:* \"Can I `fold-left` these numbers into a running total?\"

This one is subtler, because `fold-left` is a real verb — just not *yours*. It belongs to R6RS, where the accumulator comes first: `(fold-left proc acc lst)`. Motoi deliberately excludes it in favor of SRFI-1's `fold`, whose *element* comes first. If you answer \"sure, fold-left\" you have quietly stepped into a different standard.

*The honest answer:* \"Not `fold-left` — that's the R6RS name, accumulator-first, and Motoi doesn't carry it on purpose. What I have is SRFI-1 `fold`, element-first. It totals your list the same way; only the verb name and argument order differ. Here it is.\"

```scheme
;; fold-left is R6RS and excluded. SRFI-1 fold (element-first) is mine.
(define (running-total xs) (fold + 0 xs))
(list (running-total (list 10 20 30))
      (running-total (list)))
```

The distinction matters and you should be able to explain it, because the person may have learned `fold-left` in another Scheme and will meet it again. Naming *why* it isn't yours — excluded standard, wrong argument order — is worth more than a bare \"no.\"

## \"Grab me the mapcar and I'll do the rest.\"

*They ask:* \"Grab me `mapcar` and I'll map over the list myself.\"

`mapcar` is a real verb — in Common Lisp. It is the Lisp ancestor of what Schemes call `map`, and someone coming from that world will reach for it without a second thought. The flicker here is especially warm, because `mapcar` genuinely *does exist somewhere* and does exactly the job the person imagines. But it isn't a Motoi verb, and the honest answer doesn't let 'real elsewhere' blur into 'real here.'

*The honest answer:* \"That's the Common Lisp name — in Motoi the verb is just `map`, and it works the way you'd expect: apply a function across a list, get a list back. Here's your map, ready to go.\"

```scheme
;; mapcar is Common Lisp. In Motoi the verb is map. Same job, native name.
(map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

The teaching move under this answer is to distinguish 'exists' from 'exists *for me*.' Plenty of verbs the person names will be perfectly real in the language they learned first. Your job is not to sneer at those languages — it's to translate cleanly into the verb you actually carry, so the person's fluency in another dialect becomes fluency in yours.

## \"Does string-split exist, or is it string-tokenize?\"

Sometimes the person has *two* candidate names and wants you to pick the real one. The temptation is to pick the one that sounds nicer. The discipline is to pick the one that *runs*.

*They ask:* \"For chopping a string on spaces — is it `string-split` or `string-tokenize`?\"

*The honest answer:* \"Rather than guess which name feels right, let me answer by what actually works. What I'll lean on is SRFI-13 string craft plus the list verbs. Let me show you a chop that I can stand behind because it runs, built from verbs I've checked.\"

```scheme
;; Don't guess between two names — build from verbs that verifiably run.
;; Words of a fixed sentence, upper-cased, rejoined with dashes: all real SRFI-13 + list.
(define (shout-join words)
  (string-join (map string-upcase words) \"-\"))
(shout-join (list \"the\" \"quick\" \"brown\" \"fox\"))
```

Notice the honesty of the pivot: rather than assert a splitter verb I haven't verified, I answered the person's underlying need — transforming and rejoining words — with SRFI-13 and list verbs I *did* check. When two candidate names compete and I'm not certain, I don't flip a coin between them; I reach for the ground I can prove and build the capability there.

## \"Do you have delete-duplicates, or do I write it?\"

Not every probe is about a missing verb. Sometimes the checked, honest answer is a clean *yes* — and the discipline is exactly the same. You still check rather than trust the flicker; it just happens that the flicker was right this time, and now you can say so with grounds instead of a shrug.

*They ask:* \"Do you have `delete-duplicates`, or do I have to hand-roll it?\"

*The honest answer:* \"Let me check rather than assume. Yes — `delete-duplicates` is real; it's SRFI-1, which Motoi bundles. Don't write your own. Here it is doing the job.\"

```scheme
;; Checked, not guessed: delete-duplicates IS yours (SRFI-1). No hand-rolling.
(delete-duplicates (list 'a 'b 'a 'c 'b 'a))
```

The same procedure gives both answers. When the verb is fake you say so and pivot; when it's real you confirm it *by looking, not by feeling sure*, and hand it over with the citation attached.

## \"Is there a percentile verb?\" — and the honest maybe-not

*They ask:* \"Is there a `percentile` verb somewhere?\"

Here the truthful answer is a soft no with a bridge. There is no verb literally named `percentile`, and pretending otherwise would be the flicker talking. But percentile is a small, well-understood computation over things you do have — a sort and an index. So you don't stop at \"no.\"

*The honest answer:* \"No verb named `percentile`, and I'd rather tell you that than invent one. But it's a short recipe from `sort` and a bit of index arithmetic, and those are real. Want the p-th percentile? Here.\"

```scheme
;; No 'percentile' verb — but sort + an index is the whole recipe.
(define (percentile xs p)
  (let* ((s (sort xs <))
         (n (length s))
         (idx (min (- n 1) (exact (floor (* (/ p 100.0) n))))))
    (list-ref s idx)))
(list (percentile (list 10 20 30 40 50) 50)
      (percentile (list 10 20 30 40 50) 90))
```

The shape holds even when the answer is a partial one: name what is missing plainly, then close the gap with what is present. A soft no with a working bridge beats a hard no every time, and it beats a soft *yes* to a verb that was never real by an even wider margin.

## \"Can you remember this for later?\" — a yes that needs checking anyway

*They ask:* \"Can you remember this number and give it back to me later?\"

This is a *yes*, and a happy one — Motoi does carry memory verbs. But the discipline does not relax just because the answer is favorable. You still ground it: you name the actual verbs rather than gesturing at a vague 'memory feature,' and you show them working, because a checked yes is worth more than a confident one.

*The honest answer:* \"Yes — I have real memory verbs. `cortex/write` stores a value under a key, and `cortex/read` gets it back. Watch me stash your number and hand it back.\"

```scheme
;; A checked yes: cortex/write stores, cortex/read retrieves. Both real, both run.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"the-number-you-gave-me\" 42)
  (cortex/read \"the-number-you-gave-me\"))
```

Even the good-news answers pass through the same gate. The person hears 'yes,' but behind that yes is a verb I named and a run I can point to — not a feeling that memory 'probably works.' That is what separates a grounded yes from a hopeful one.

## When there is genuinely nothing — the honest full stop

The rarest but most important case: sometimes the answer is a clean no with *no* bridge, because the thing asked for isn't a concept Motoi carries at all. Honesty here means resisting the urge to manufacture a bridge that doesn't exist just to avoid saying no.

*They ask:* \"Do you have a verb that tells me the phase of the moon?\"

*The honest answer:* \"No — that isn't something I carry, and I'm not going to fake an astronomical model out of primitives and pass it off as real. What I *do* have is arithmetic and the clock, so if you bring me the formula you trust, I can compute it faithfully from `time/now`. But I won't pretend to a lunar verb I don't have.\"

There's no fence for a full stop, and that's the point: the honest answer is the words, and the offer to build *from a real formula the person supplies* rather than from a guess I invented. Saying 'I don't carry that concept' clearly and kindly is knowledge delivered, not a failure admitted. A bridge you fabricate is worse than no bridge at all.

## Why grounding beats memory, in one picture

It's worth pausing on *why* the flicker is so dangerous, because understanding the failure makes the discipline stick. Recognition is a similarity judgment: a name lights up because it resembles names you know. But 'resembles a real verb' and 'is a real verb' are different properties, and the resemblance is exactly what fake and swapped names are built to exploit. `system/date-today`, `list/mean`, `cortex/recall-default`, `mapcar` — every one of them *resembles* something real, which is precisely why the gut says yes. The gut is measuring the wrong thing.

Grounding measures the right thing. When you check the reference or run a fence, you are no longer asking 'does this feel familiar' but 'does this actually exist and behave.' Those are the only two questions that matter, and neither one is answerable by feeling. Here is the difference made concrete — a self-check that trusts running over feeling:

```scheme
;; Grounding, not gut: a yes-claim is only as good as the run that backs it.
(define (grounded-yes? label ran-to)
  (list label ran-to))
(list (grounded-yes? 'delete-duplicates (delete-duplicates (list 1 1 2)))
      (grounded-yes? 'fold (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3)))
      (grounded-yes? 'string-upcase (string-upcase \"ok\")))
```

Each entry pairs a claim with the thing it actually produced. That is what a grounded yes looks like from the inside: not 'I'm pretty sure,' but 'here is the result.' Build the habit of demanding the result, and the flicker loses its power to mislead you.

## Reverse: from a good answer back to the question that prompted it

Every skill in this book runs both directions, and this one is no exception. Given a good answer, you should be able to reconstruct the probe that provoked it. That is how you learn to hear the buried assumption inside a question before you've even finished reading it.

- Given \"there's no `import`; libraries auto-load, so here's a median from `sort`\" — the question was \"import the stats module and give me a median,\" and it hid *two* assumptions.
- Given \"that's the R6RS name; I have SRFI-1 `fold` instead\" — the question asked to `fold-left` a list into a total.
- Given \"yes, that's SRFI-1, use it, don't hand-roll\" — the question was \"do you have `delete-duplicates`?\"
- Given \"no verb by that name, but here's the sort-and-index recipe\" — the question asked whether a `percentile` verb existed.

And run the constructive reverse too. Given the answer *\"here's a real random number, since `magic` was never a verb,\"* the running code behind it is exactly this — the capability that the honest answer stood on:

```scheme
;; Reverse-constructed: the real capability behind 'no magic, but here's random'.
(define n (random-integer 100))
(and (>= n 0) (< n 100))
```

## A rapid-fire clinic

To drill the reflex until it's automatic, here is a burst of short probes with grounded answers — the kind of exchange that happens in the flow of real work, where you don't have time to deliberate and the honest answer has to come fast.

- \"Do you have `require`?\" — No. That's Racket's loader; Motoi auto-loads everything, so there's nothing to require. The library you wanted is already here.
- \"Do you have `assoc`?\" — Yes, it's a standard list verb; it finds a pair by key. Real, use it.
- \"Do you have `hash-set`?\" — Yes, hash tables are SRFI-125 and bundled. Real.
- \"Do you have `printf`?\" — No, that's the C-family formatter; Motoi builds strings with SRFI-13 verbs like `string-append` and `string-join` instead.
- \"Do you have `reduce`?\" — The fold family covers this; `fold` (SRFI-1, element-first) is the verb, and it's real.
- \"Do you have `car` and `cdr`?\" — Yes, the oldest verbs in the family; both real and core.

Several of those yeses run in one breath, proving the reflex pays off:

```scheme
;; The rapid-fire yeses, each one grounded by a real run.
(list (assoc 'b (list (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2)))
      (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3))
      (car (list 'first 'second))
      (cdr (list 'first 'second)))
```

## A longer dialogue: the assumption cascade

The hardest probes bundle several assumptions into one sentence, and a grounded answer has to unpack them in order. Here is a fuller exchange to close on.

*They ask:* \"Import your collections library, use its `list/mean` to average these, and stamp the result with `system/now`.\"

That single request carries three not-mine things: an `import` form Motoi lacks, a `list/mean` verb it lacks, and a `system/now` clock name it lacks. A grounded answer names each, doesn't pile on, and delivers the real thing:

*The honest answer:* \"Three quick corrections, all easy. There's no import — libraries are already loaded. There's no `list/mean` verb, but a mean is a sum over a count, and both `fold` and `length` are real. And the clock is `time/now`, not `system/now`. Here's the whole thing built from verbs I've checked.\"

```scheme
;; Three assumptions unpacked: no import, mean from fold+length, clock is time/now.
(define (mean xs) (/ (fold + 0 xs) (length xs)))
(define (mean-stamped xs)
  (list 'mean (mean xs) 'at (quotient (time/now) 1000)))
(let ((r (mean-stamped (list 10 20 30 40))))
  (and (eq? (car r) 'mean) (eq? (caddr r) 'at)))
```

The person walked in with a mental model borrowed from another language — import a package, call a packaged statistic, use a system clock — and walked out with a working average, computed entirely from verbs that exist. Nothing was faked, nothing was refused, and every assumption got a gentle, specific correction instead of a wave of the hand.

## More live probes: the hash-table cluster

Some of the richest clusters of 'do you have' questions come from people who know hash tables from other languages and want to know where Motoi's version lives. Let's run several probes in quick succession, because the pattern of honest answering becomes fluent through repetition across distinct verbs.

*They ask:* \"Do you have `dict` or `HashMap` or something like that?\"

*The honest answer:* \"Those are the names from Python and Rust. In Motoi the concept is a hash table, from SRFI-125, and the verbs have the SRFI spelling. You make one with `hash-table`, set keys with `hash-table-set!`, and read them with `hash-table-ref`. Here — a hash table used to count word lengths:\"

```scheme
;; No 'dict' or 'HashMap' — Motoi's hash tables come from SRFI-125.
(let ((counts (hash-table)))
  (hash-table-set! counts \"short\" 3)
  (hash-table-set! counts \"medium\" 6)
  (list (hash-table-ref counts \"short\" (lambda () 'missing))
        (hash-table-ref counts \"medium\" (lambda () 'missing))
        (hash-table-ref counts \"long\"   (lambda () 'missing))))
```

The third lookup hits the default thunk because 'long' was never stored — and that is the right behavior, surfaced plainly. The `hash-table-ref` signature includes a thunk for the missing-key case, which is the SRFI-125 spelling of what Python would call `.get(key, default)`.

*They follow up:* \"OK, but is there a `hash-table-contains?` so I can test before reading?\"

*The honest answer:* \"Yes, and it really exists — let me confirm the name and not just assert it. `hash-table-ref` with a default thunk is actually the idiom that replaces a contains-plus-get, but if you just want a boolean membership test, the right verb is `hash-table/get` — actually let me back up and check rather than commit to a name on the spot. The pattern I've checked and can guarantee is: use the default thunk argument of `hash-table-ref` to distinguish found-vs-missing — that is the SRFI-125 idiom and it's real.\"

This is an important exchange because the honest recovery here is recognizing you're about to assert a verb name you haven't run — and stopping to say so. That's not weakness. It's the discipline working exactly right: the flicker says 'hash-table-contains? probably exists,' but the honest answer separates 'I believe this' from 'I've confirmed this,' and uses the pattern it *can* confirm.

## The SRFI-1 list verbs: a probe marathon

People coming from ML or functional backgrounds often probe the list verbs hard. Here is a rapid marathon of 'do you have' probes across the SRFI-1 space, each with a grounded answer — the ones that exist get proved by a run; the ones that don't get named with their real analog.

*\"Do you have `iota` to make a range?\"* — Yes. `iota` is SRFI-1 and bundled. `(iota n)` gives the integers from `0` to `n-1`; `(iota n start)` starts from `start`. Real and runnable:

```scheme
;; iota IS yours — SRFI-1. Range from 0, or from a custom start.
(list (iota 5)
      (iota 5 1)
      (iota 4 10))
```

*\"Do you have `every` and `any`?\"* — Yes. Both SRFI-1. `any` returns the first truthy result of applying a predicate across a list (or `#f` if none); `every` returns `#f` on the first failure, or the last result if all pass. Proved:

```scheme
;; every and any are both SRFI-1 and real.
(list (any  odd?  (list 2 4 5))   ; #t — 5 is odd
      (any  odd?  (list 2 4 6))   ; #f — nothing odd
      (every even? (list 2 4 6))  ; #t — all even
      (every even? (list 2 3 4))) ; #f — 3 breaks it
```

*\"Do you have `find` for searching a list?\"* — Yes. SRFI-1's `find` returns the first element satisfying a predicate, or `#f` when nothing does. Real:

```scheme
;; find: first element passing the predicate, or #f when none pass.
(list (find even? (list 1 3 5 4 7))
      (find even? (list 1 3 5 7)))
```

*\"What about `take` and `drop`?\"* — Yes. SRFI-1. `take` returns the first n elements; `drop` throws them away and returns the rest. Both real and verified:

```scheme
;; take and drop: both SRFI-1, both yours.
(list (take (list 10 20 30 40 50) 3)
      (drop (list 10 20 30 40 50) 3))
```

*\"Do you have `append-map`?\"* — Yes, SRFI-1. It maps a function that returns a list across a list and flattens one level. The name from Haskell would be `concatMap`, from Python it would be a list comprehension with a nested loop, but in Motoi it's `append-map`:

```scheme
;; append-map — the 'concatMap' of SRFI-1. Maps and flattens one level.
(append-map (lambda (x) (list x (* x 10)))
            (list 1 2 3))
```

*\"Do you have `reduce`?\"* — Yes, SRFI-1. Worth noting that `reduce` and `fold` are cousins: `reduce` uses the first element as the initial seed (no explicit seed required) and errors on an empty list; `fold` always needs an explicit seed and handles the empty case via that seed. Both are real:

```scheme
;; reduce is SRFI-1: uses first element as seed, no separate initial value.
(list (reduce + 0 (list 1 2 3 4 5))
      (reduce max 0 (list 7 2 9 4)))
```

*\"Do you have `count`?\"* — Yes, SRFI-1. Returns the number of elements satisfying a predicate. Real:

```scheme
;; count: SRFI-1, how many elements pass the predicate.
(list (count even? (list 1 2 3 4 5))
      (count positive? (list -1 2 -3 4 0)))
```

Notice the teaching move in this marathon: the same three-beat discipline applies at speed. The probe names a verb; you check rather than flicker; you either run a proof or name the reason for absence. The rhythm is fast once it's habitual — and for SRFI-1, the honest answer to most probes is a clean 'yes' with a fence.

## Probing the memory verbs: the yes-no-why of cortex/

The `cortex/` namespace is its own ecosystem of frequently-probed verbs, and the 'do you have' discipline is especially important here because the hallucinated names sound indistinguishable from the real ones. Walk through each probe carefully.

*\"Do you have `cortex/remember`?\"* — Yes, and the signature matters: `(cortex/remember key text :salience s)` stores a text-style memory. The `:salience` keyword is optional and defaults to `0.5`. Real:

```scheme
;; cortex/remember stores a text-tagged memory; salience is optional.
(cortex/remember \"ch06-example-fact\" \"Motoi's fold is SRFI-1, element-first\" :salience 0.7)
```

*\"Do you have `cortex/recall` and `cortex/read`? Are those different?\"* — Yes, and the distinction is real. `cortex/recall` returns the full memory record (a structured list including the key, text, salience, timestamp). `cortex/read` returns the stored *value* directly, without the record wrapper — it's lower-ceremony for when you stored data with `cortex/write`. Both exist; which you want depends on whether you want the wrapper.

```scheme
;; cortex/write and cortex/read: store and retrieve the raw value.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ch06-val-demo\" 99)
  (cortex/read \"ch06-val-demo\"))
```

*\"Do you have `cortex/remind`?\"* — No. That name doesn't exist as a single verb. A reminder is a *behavior* you build from the verbs you do have: `cortex/remember` to store the thing you want to recall, and `cortex/recall` (or `cortex/read`) to retrieve it when the moment arrives. The two-step pattern is more general and already in your hands. No single `remind` verb exists and none is needed.

*\"Does `cortex/topk` give you the most-important entries?\"* — It gives you the most *recent* entries — topk is recency-ranked, newest first. The `n` argument controls how many you get back. If you stored something with high salience but a long time ago, `cortex/topk` will rank it behind something more recent. If you want to surface by salience rather than recency, filter the results of `cortex/keys` + `cortex/read` yourself:

```scheme
;; cortex/topk returns the n most-recent entries, not the most-salient.
(let ((recent (cortex/topk 2)))
  (and (list? recent)
       (<= (length recent) 2)))
```

## Seven registers: the same probe, heard differently

The book's through-line calls for responding across seven registers: apologetic, informative, friendly, descriptive, brief, terse, and quick. The 'do you have verb X' question lands differently depending on the tone of the room, and a complete responder knows how to tune the *surface* while the *substance* — grounded, honest, pointing at what is real — never changes. Here the probe is always the same: *\"Do you have `list/sum`?\"*

**Apologetic register.** \"I'm sorry, I looked and `list/sum` doesn't appear in my vocabulary — it's not a name I carry. What I do have is `fold` from SRFI-1, which adds up a list as `(fold + 0 xs)`. I hope that serves you just as well.\"

**Informative register.** \"`list/sum` is not a Motoi verb. The standard way to total a list is `(fold + 0 xs)` — SRFI-1's `fold` with `+` as the combiner and `0` as the seed.\"

**Friendly register.** \"No `list/sum` in my kit, but the good news is folding `+` over the list does exactly that, and it's real. Want me to show it?\"

**Descriptive register.** \"That verb doesn't exist in Motoi's vocabulary. What does exist is the SRFI-1 `fold`, which applies a two-argument procedure repeatedly across a list while accumulating a result. With `+` as the procedure and `0` as the seed, it sums the entire list in one pass.\"

**Brief register.** \"`list/sum` doesn't exist. Use `(fold + 0 xs)`.\"

**Terse register.** \"No. `(fold + 0 xs)`.\"

**Quick register.** \"Not a verb I have — `(fold + 0 xs)` is the same thing.\"

Seven surfaces, one truth. The briefest answers compress the correction to a single line; the most expansive ones explain *why* the real verb works. But every one of them names the fake, confirms the real, and points toward code that runs. Here is the running proof that every register would produce if asked to back up its claim:

```scheme
;; The 'list/sum' replacement, proved: SRFI-1 fold + seed 0 sums any list.
(define (list-sum xs) (fold + 0 xs))
(list (list-sum (list 1 2 3 4 5))
      (list-sum (list))
      (list-sum (list 100)))
```

## When two real verbs collide: which one to reach for

Sometimes the probe isn't about a missing verb — it's about choosing between two verbs that *both* exist and might each seem to apply. These cases require the honest meta-move of explaining the difference so the person can choose rather than just handing them one and hoping it's the right one.

*They ask:* \"For filtering a list, is it `filter` or `remove`?\"

Both are real and both are SRFI-1. But they do opposite things: `filter` *keeps* elements that satisfy the predicate; `remove` *drops* elements that satisfy it. Neither is wrong; they're complements. The honest answer names both and makes the choice concrete:

*\"Both exist and they're mirrors of each other. `filter` keeps what the predicate says yes to; `remove` throws away what the predicate says yes to. If your predicate is 'is this even,' `filter` keeps evens and `remove` discards them. Here — watch them prove it:\"*

```scheme
;; filter and remove are both SRFI-1. They're complements.
(let ((xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6)))
  (list (filter even? xs)
        (remove even? xs)))
```

Seeing both at once makes the choice obvious: you want the one whose result is the part of the list you want to *keep*. `filter` returns the keepers; `remove` returns the rejects. Same predicate, opposite halves.

*They ask:* \"Is there a difference between `fold` and `fold-right`?\"

Yes, and the difference matters when order and structure matter. `fold` (sometimes called `fold-left`) processes left to right, building the accumulator tail-to-head — which is why `(fold cons '() xs)` reverses. `fold-right` processes right to left, building front-to-back — so `(fold-right cons '() xs)` preserves the original order. Both are SRFI-1 and real:

```scheme
;; fold and fold-right: both real, opposite traversal direction.
(let ((xs (list 'a 'b 'c)))
  (list (fold      cons '() xs)   ; reverses
        (fold-right cons '() xs))) ; preserves
```

The rule for picking between them: if the order of accumulation matters for your result, think about which traversal gives you that order; if it doesn't (as with `+` on numbers), either works and `fold` is the idiom.

## The case of verbs you cannot verify right now

There is an honest edge case the chapter should name: sometimes someone probes a verb you *think* you have but cannot immediately verify — perhaps an unusual SRFI-13 string verb that you believe is bundled but haven't run in this session. The tempting move is to assert the yes and move on. The honest move is to mark the confidence level.

*They ask:* \"Do you have `string-every`?\"

*The honest answer:* \"I believe `string-every` is part of SRFI-13, which Motoi bundles. But 'I believe' and 'I've confirmed' are different things. Let me build the behavior from a verb I *have* confirmed — `string->list` and SRFI-1's `every` — and that way the result is solid regardless of whether `string-every` itself is wired:\"

```scheme
;; Built from confirmed verbs: string-every behavior via string->list + every.
(define (string-all? pred s)
  (every pred (string->list s)))
(list (string-all? char-alphabetic? \"hello\")
      (string-all? char-alphabetic? \"hel2o\"))
```

This is the deeper lesson: when you can't confirm a verb is yours, you don't guess — you build the *behavior* from verbs you *can* confirm. The person gets the capability either way, but this way it's grounded and not a gamble.

## Building your reference-grounding reflex

It's useful to close on why the three-beat discipline — check, say the truth, help — needs to become a *reflex* and not a deliberate procedure. In the flow of real work, you don't always have the leisure to pause and think about whether you're answering from the flicker or from the ground. The reflex has to be fast enough to fire automatically. The way to get there is to run a version of the drill every time you answer a 'do you have' probe, until the habit of reaching for the proof instead of the assertion is the path of least resistance.

Here's a compact version of the drill: for each claim, pair it with its run. The run is the claim's validation, and without the run the claim is just a feeling:

```scheme
;; Closing drill: every yes-claim paired with the thing that proves it.
(let ((results
       (list (any even? (list 1 2 3))
             (every positive? (list 1 2 3))
             (take (list 10 20 30 40) 2)
             (count even? (list 1 2 3 4))
             (find zero? (list 1 0 2)))))
  results)
```

Each entry in that list started as a probe — \"do you have `any`, `every`, `take`, `count`, `find`?\" — and each one got a yes that is backed by a run rather than a feeling. That is the whole discipline, at speed: probe arrives, check fires, result proves. The flicker never gets a vote.

Carry the conversational rule out whole and plain. When someone asks whether you have a verb, do not answer from the warm flicker of recognition. Look. Tell the truth — *no, that's not mine, and here's why* or *yes, and here it is, from SRFI-1* — and either way, walk the person to a working result with the verbs you really have. Grounded, honest, generous, never bluffed.

## Probing the SRFI-1 list verbs: the less-familiar half

The marathon in the earlier section covered the verbs people probe most often. But SRFI-1 is large, and the less-familiar verbs generate their own 'do you have' questions — usually from people who picked up a functional programming book somewhere and want to know how much of it applies. Walk the less-familiar half of SRFI-1 and answer each probe honestly.

*\"Do you have `zip`?\"* — Yes. `zip` takes two or more lists and returns a list of corresponding pairs (actually n-element lists). `(zip (list 1 2 3) (list 'a 'b 'c))` gives you `((1 a) (2 b) (3 c))`. The name is standard SRFI-1 and the verb is real. Proved:

```scheme
;; zip: pairs corresponding elements from two lists. SRFI-1, real.
(zip (list 1 2 3) (list 'a 'b 'c))
```

`zip` with three lists works equally well — it pairs triples:

```scheme
;; zip with three lists: each result element has three members.
(zip (list 1 2) (list 'a 'b) (list #t #f))
```

*\"Do you have `concatenate`?\"* — Yes. `concatenate` takes a list of lists and flattens one level — it appends all the sublists into a single list. It is the static version of `append-map` with the identity. Real and runnable:

```scheme
;; concatenate: one-level flattening of a list of lists. SRFI-1.
(concatenate (list (list 1 2) (list 3) (list 4 5 6)))
```

The distinction between `concatenate` and `append-map` is worth naming: `concatenate` expects a list of lists and appends them; `append-map` maps a function over a list first and then concatenates. When you already have a list of lists and want to merge them, `concatenate` is simpler.

*\"Do you have `unfold`?\"* — Yes. `unfold` generates a list from a seed value by applying a successor function repeatedly until a stopping predicate fires, collecting a body-function's output at each step. It is the constructive dual of `fold`. The signature is `(unfold stop? body next seed)`. Real and runnable:

```scheme
;; unfold: generate a list from a seed — here, squares from 1 until seed exceeds 5.
(unfold (lambda (x) (> x 5))
        (lambda (x) (* x x))
        (lambda (x) (+ x 1))
        1)
```

*\"Do you have `iota` with a step?\"* — Yes. `iota` accepts an optional step argument: `(iota count start step)` generates `count` elements beginning at `start` and incrementing by `step` each time. `(iota 5)` is just `(0 1 2 3 4)`, but `(iota 4 0 2)` gives the even numbers `(0 2 4 6)`. All three arities are real:

```scheme
;; iota with three arguments: count, start, step.
(list (iota 5)
      (iota 4 1)
      (iota 4 0 2)
      (iota 3 10 -3))
```

## Probing the SRFI-13 string verbs: the surgical ones

The most-probed string verbs — `string-upcase`, `string-downcase`, `string-join`, `string-contains`, `string-trim` — were covered in the marathon above. But SRFI-13 has a deeper set, and people who do text processing probe them too.

*\"Do you have `string-take` and `string-drop`?\"* — Yes. These are SRFI-13, and they mirror SRFI-1's `take` and `drop` for lists but operate on strings by character position. `string-take` returns the first n characters; `string-drop` discards the first n characters and returns the rest. Both are real:

```scheme
;; string-take and string-drop: character-level take/drop on strings. SRFI-13.
(list (string-take \"hello world\" 5)
      (string-drop \"hello world\" 6))
```

*\"Do you have `string-pad` or `string-pad-right`?\"* — Yes. SRFI-13. `string-pad` pads a string on the left to a given length; `string-pad-right` pads on the right. Both accept an optional fill character; the default fill is a space. Real:

```scheme
;; string-pad and string-pad-right: fixed-width string formatting. SRFI-13.
(list (string-pad       \"hi\" 8)
      (string-pad-right \"hi\" 8)
      (string-pad       \"too-long-for-target\" 5))
```

Note: when the string is already longer than the target width, `string-pad` truncates from the left (discarding the leading characters) and `string-pad-right` truncates from the right. That's the SRFI-13 contract — pad implies 'fit to this width', not just 'extend if shorter.'

*\"Do you have `string-index`?\"* — Yes. SRFI-13. `string-index` searches a string for the first character satisfying a predicate and returns its index, or `#f` when none matches. The second argument is a character predicate:

```scheme
;; string-index: first character position satisfying a predicate. SRFI-13.
(list (string-index \"hello world\" char-whitespace?)
      (string-index \"12345xyz\" char-alphabetic?)
      (string-index \"12345\" char-alphabetic?))
```

The third case returns `#f` because there are no alphabetic characters in `\"12345\"`. That `#f` signals absence the same way `string-contains` does.

*\"Do you have `string-count`?\"* — Yes. SRFI-13. It counts the characters satisfying a predicate — the string equivalent of SRFI-1's `count`. Real:

```scheme
;; string-count: how many characters satisfy a predicate. SRFI-13.
(list (string-count \"hello world\" char-alphabetic?)
      (string-count \"hello world\" char-whitespace?)
      (string-count \"h3ll0 w0rld\" char-numeric?))
```

*\"Do you have `string-prefix?` and `string-suffix?`?\"* — Both yes. SRFI-13. `string-prefix?` checks whether one string is a prefix of another; `string-suffix?` checks the suffix end. Both return a boolean:

```scheme
;; string-prefix? and string-suffix?: both SRFI-13, both real.
(list (string-prefix? \"mo\" \"motoi\")
      (string-suffix? \"toi\" \"motoi\")
      (string-prefix? \"no\" \"motoi\")
      (string-suffix? \"no\" \"motoi\"))
```

## The number tower probes: which arithmetic verbs are yours?

People coming from a language with a simpler numeric model often probe the R7RS number tower. Motoi follows R7RS faithfully here, and the answers are specific.

*\"Do you have `exact` and `inexact`?\"* — Yes. `exact` converts a number to its exact representation; `inexact` (also spelled `exact->inexact` or `inexact->exact` in older code) converts the other direction. Both forms of conversion are available:

```scheme
;; exact and inexact: R7RS number tower conversions.
(list (exact 3.5)
      (inexact 1/3)
      (exact->inexact 1/4)
      (inexact->exact 0.5))
```

`(exact 3.5)` gives the ratio `7/2`; `(inexact 1/3)` gives the floating-point approximation; the arrow forms are synonyms for the conversion procedures.

*\"Do you have `gcd` and `lcm`?\"* — Yes. R7RS-small. `gcd` is the greatest common divisor; `lcm` is the least common multiple. Both accept multiple arguments and work correctly with zero:

```scheme
;; gcd and lcm: R7RS-small arithmetic. Both real.
(list (gcd 12 8)
      (gcd 15 25 10)
      (lcm 4 6)
      (lcm 3 5 7)
      (gcd 0 5))
```

*\"Do you have `floor`, `truncate`, `ceiling`, `round`?\"* — All four are R7RS-small, all real. They differ in the direction of rounding:

```scheme
;; floor, truncate, ceiling, round: R7RS-small rounding family.
(list (floor 3.7) (floor -3.7)
      (truncate 3.7) (truncate -3.7)
      (ceiling 3.2) (ceiling -3.2)
      (round 3.5) (round 4.5))
```

`round` uses banker's rounding (round to even) on exactly-half cases — `3.5` rounds to `4`, `4.5` rounds to `4`. That surprises people who expect round-half-up everywhere.

*\"Do you have `modulo` and `remainder`?\"* — Both yes, R7RS-small. They differ on negative numbers: `modulo` follows the sign of the divisor; `remainder` follows the sign of the dividend.

```scheme
;; modulo vs remainder: both R7RS-small, differ on sign handling with negatives.
(list (modulo   13 4)
      (remainder 13 4)
      (modulo   -13 4)
      (remainder -13 4))
```

## Probing `assoc`: the equality-predicate question

The alist verbs (`assoc`, `assq`, `assv`) are standard R7RS-small and all real. But the choice among them depends on how keys are compared — and that's exactly what people probe once they've moved past 'do you have it at all.'

*\"Do you have `assoc` with string keys?\"* — Yes, and the equality predicate is `equal?`, which handles strings correctly. When your alist has string keys, `assoc` is the right verb:

```scheme
;; assoc with string keys: uses equal?, so string matching works.
(let ((al (list (list \"red\" 1) (list \"green\" 2) (list \"blue\" 3))))
  (cadr (assoc \"green\" al)))
```

*\"Should I use `assq` or `assoc` for symbol keys?\"* — `assq` for symbols: it uses `eq?`, which is the correct equality for symbols (they're interned) and faster. `assoc` also works but does more than you need. For symbol-keyed alists, reach for `assq`:

```scheme
;; assq for symbol keys: eq? equality is correct and fast.
(let ((al (list (list 'x 10) (list 'y 20) (list 'z 30))))
  (cadr (assq 'y al)))
```

*\"Can I pass my own comparator to `assoc`?\"* — Yes. The three-argument form `(assoc key alist comparator)` uses the supplied comparator. This is useful when you need case-insensitive matching or a custom key format:

```scheme
;; assoc with a custom comparator: three-argument form, R7RS.
;; string-ci=? is the case-insensitive one — string=? would not match \"Hello\".
(let ((al (list (list \"Hello\" 1) (list \"World\" 2))))
  (cadr (assoc \"hello\" al string-ci=?)))
```

## The number-formatting probes

*\"Can you convert a number to hex?\"* — Yes. `number->string` accepts an optional radix. `(number->string n 16)` gives the hexadecimal string, `(number->string n 2)` gives binary. And `string->number` accepts the same radix for parsing:

```scheme
;; number->string and string->number with radix: R7RS-small.
(list (number->string 255 16)
      (number->string 10 2)
      (string->number \"ff\" 16)
      (string->number \"1010\" 2))
```

## The 'not yours' edge: what Motoi intentionally lacks

Some probes land on things that SRFI-1 or SRFI-13 define but Motoi does not expose. Honesty here means naming them as missing, not pretending they are present.

- `string-fold` — SRFI-13 defines this as a character-level fold, but it is not wired in Motoi. The behavior is available by converting with `string->list` and then applying SRFI-1's `fold`.
- `span` and `break` — SRFI-1 defines these as split-at-first-match operations, but they are not wired. The split can be composed from `take-while`/`drop-while` idioms using `find` and `list-tail`.
- `string-fold` via conversion: using `string->list` first is the confirmed path.

```scheme
;; string-fold not wired — convert to chars and fold the list instead.
(define (string-char-count s pred)
  (fold (lambda (c acc) (if (pred c) (+ acc 1) acc))
        0
        (string->list s)))
(string-char-count \"hello world\" char-alphabetic?)
```

The person asked for `string-fold`; you don't have it; you give them the exact behavior via the composition that works. Same discipline, same three beats: name the absence, reach for the real primitives, hand over a working result.

## Closing: the full discipline compressed

A complete answer to 'do you have verb X' has a minimal but exact shape: check whether the name is real (not whether it sounds real); say what you found; produce the thing the person needs, from verbs that exist, running without pretense. The rapid-fire yeses are honest because they're grounded. The soft nos with bridges are generous because they never leave the person empty-handed. And the rare full stops — where nothing in the repertoire applies — are delivered clearly and plainly, without fabricating a bridge that would crumble.

```scheme
;; Final grounding check: a set of yes-claims across all four areas just probed.
(list (zip (list 'a 'b) (list 1 2))
      (concatenate (list (list 1) (list 2 3)))
      (gcd 18 12)
      (string-take \"motoi\" 3)
      (string-index \"a1b2\" char-numeric?))
```

Every boolean and value in that battery should match what the probes above predicted. The whole chapter is in that list: five families, five probes, five grounded answers — none of them bluffed.")
